<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mensheviks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mensheviks were the moderate faction of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party, formed when it split with Lenin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in 1903. Led by Julius Martov and others, they favoured a broad party and a bourgeois-democratic stage before socialism. They took part in the Provisional Government of 1917, governed independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1918 to 1921 and were suppressed by the Soviet regime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mensheviks.jpg" alt="Mensheviks" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mensevikii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mensheviks were a faction of the Marxist Russian Social Democratic Labour Party that split with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>'s Bolsheviks at the Second Party Congress in August 1903. Julius Martov and Lenin disagreed over the editorial committee of the party newspaper Iskra and over the definition of a party member. Lenin's formulation required membership of one of the party's organisations, while Martov's required only work under the guidance of one. Lenin argued for a small party of professional revolutionaries, Martov for a large party of activists with broad representation.</p><p>Martov's definition was accepted by 28 votes to 23. But seven delegates then left, five of them representatives of the Jewish Bund who protested the defeat of their own federalist proposal, and Lenin's supporters gained a slight majority, which decided the names of the factions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The 4th Congress of 1906 formally reunited the party, with the Mensheviks in the majority, yet Lenin's definition of a party member replaced Martov's. The two factions kept separate structures. Both held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was too undeveloped for socialism, so that the revolution against the tsarist regime would be bourgeois-democratic. After 1905 the Mensheviks were more inclined to work with liberal parties such as the Constitutional Democrats, and argued for legal methods and trade union work, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> favoured armed violence and alliances with peasant representatives.</p><p>Georgi Plekhanov's position that a period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> had to come before a socialist revolution was associated with the Mensheviks. Alexander Potresov called for the party to suspend illegal work, and in 1908 Lenin called Mensheviks who left for legal organisations liquidationists. Leaders included Martov and Pavel Axelrod. The party split formally in 1912. The Menshevik faction split further in 1917: most opposed the war, but a vocal minority supported it as national defence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mensheviks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In June 1918 the <b>Mensheviks</b> and the Socialist Revolutionaries were expelled from the soviets, and after the Left Socialist Revolutionaries, briefly the Bolsheviks’ partners, rose in July 1918, they too were suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mensheviks</b> and social democrats thought Russia too backward for a socialist revolution; council and left communists held that the Bolsheviks had built state capitalism, and Trotsky called the Soviet Union under Stalin a degenerated workers’ state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Socialist Revolutionaries, <b>Mensheviks</b> and Armenian Dashnaks formed a new government, named after the Centrocaspian committee of sailors of the Caspian Fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/centrocaspian-dictatorship">Centrocaspian Dictatorship</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed by the Social Democratic (<b>Menshevik</b>) party under Noe Zhordania, the republic held democratic elections, carried out land reform, founded Tbilisi State University in 1918 and adopted a constitution in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-georgia">Democratic Republic of Georgia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Bolsheviks seized power in Petrograd in November 1917, Georgian <b>Mensheviks</b>, Armenian Dashnaks and Azerbaijani Musavatists in Tiflis refused to accept their rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-commissariat">Transcaucasian Commissariat</a></li><li><blockquote>The government was headed by the Georgian <b>Menshevik</b> Akaki Chkhenkeli, with Nikolay Chkheidze chairing the Sejm.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-democratic-federative-republic">Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic</a></li></ul></section>
